      "claim": "Paltusotine, the highest-transfer analogue, showed 69% absolute oral bioavailability in humans after a 20 mg oral solution compared with IV tracer, directly supporting high F for a related azabicyclic heteroaromatic molecule.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C19"],
      "claim": "Moxifloxacin, a high-transfer analogue with an ionizable amine and aromatic heterocycles, showed approximately 90% oral bioavailability in healthy volunteers, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C17"],
      "claim": "Prasugrel, a high-transfer analogue, showed about 79% oral bioavailability in humans, supporting high F despite potential metabolic liability.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C15"],
      "claim": "Brigatinib, a high-transfer analogue, showed 46% oral bioavailability in healthy volunteers, still well above the 20% threshold and supportive of high classification.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C26"],
      "claim": "Apixaban, a high-transfer analogue, showed approximately 50% oral bioavailability in humans, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "All retrieved analogues with high assay-transfer scores report oral bioavailability values of 41–92%, all above the 20% threshold. The query's moderate size, ionizable amine, and aromatic heterocycle are consistent with good absorption, so high bioavailability is predicted.",
